=== text ===
The sky appears blue because sunlight, which is white light containing all colors of the visible spectrum (red through violet), consists of many wavelengths. As this white light passes through Earth's atmosphere, molecules in the air scatter shorter wavelengths—such as red and orange—less effectively than shorter wavelengths like blue and blue-violet. Consequently, a small portion of each color reaches your eye; however, since blue and blue-green light are scattered much more strongly by gas molecules (a phenomenon known as Rayleigh scattering) due to their interaction with the molecule's size relative to the wavelength, our sky appears blue during the day. This is why we see a clear blue sky on a sunny day.
