=== text ===
The sky appears blue because sunlight, which is made of all colors of the visible spectrum, interacts with gas molecules in Earth's atmosphere through a process called **Rayleigh scattering**.

Sunlight consists of all colors of the visible spectrum. When this light encounters air molecules (mostly nitrogen and nitrogen), smaller particles or smaller particles scatter shorter wavelengths of light more strongly than longer wavelengths. Since blue light has a shorter wavelength and is scattered much more intensely than red or yellow light, our eyes perceive the sky as blue during the day. This is why we see the sky as blue; at sunset or sunset, when the sun is lower on the horizon, the light must travel through more atmosphere to reach your eyes, scattering most of the blue light away and leaving only the deep reds and oranges visible.
