=== text ===
The sky appears blue because sunlight, which is white light containing all colors of the visible spectrum (red through violet), consists of many wavelengths. As this light passes through Earth's atmosphere, shorter wavelengths—such as red and orange—are scattered less than shorter wavelengths like blue and violet due to their longer wavelengths? No wait—the scattering intensity ($I$) depends on wavelength $\lambda$. According to Rayleigh's law, the amount of scattering is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength: $I \propto 1/\lambda^4$. Since red light (longer wavelengths) has a lower frequency and longer wavelengths than blue or violet light, it scatters less effectively. Conversely, shorter wavelengths like blue and violet are scattered much more strongly because their shorter wavelengths interact more efficiently with atmospheric molecules through Rayleigh scattering. Thus, when white sunlight enters the atmosphere, all colors scatter some amount of energy back toward Earth's surface, but only a small fraction reaches our eyes directly from high altitudes or clouds; instead, most of this light is reflected upward into space where it can be scattered again before reaching an observer on the ground.

In simpler terms: The sky appears blue because while sunlight contains all colors, shorter wavelengths (blue/violet) are scattered more strongly by air molecules than longer-wavelength red/orange light due to Rayleigh scattering physics. This is why our skies appear blue during daylight hours and why sunsets appear reddish—when the Sun sets or sets behind clouds
