=== text ===
The sky appears blue primarily due to a phenomenon known as **Rayleigh scattering**. When sunlight interacts with the atmosphere, it consists of light waves across various wavelengths (colors), ranging from violet and red on opposite ends of the spectrum.

Air molecules scatter shorter-wavelength colors—such as blues and violets—much more efficiently than they do longer-wavelength redder light because these photons interact more frequently with air molecules as their frequency increases ([Rayleigh scattering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scattering_(physics))). Since blue and violet wavelengths have higher frequencies, the Earth's atmosphere scatters them much more strongly. Although red is also present in sunlight, our eyes are evolutionarily tuned to perceive it less sharply than other colors; we generally see white or gray instead of a rainbow spectrum because our visual system interprets this mix as "white" light rather than pure red or blue hues.

**Final Answer:**
Because the sun emits all visible wavelengths with roughly equal intensity, and human eyes are most sensitive to greenish-yellow waves (which is why they appear brightest), we see white daylight under normal conditions. However, if you were to look directly at a clear sky on an overcast day or at noon in direct sunlight without clouds blocking your view of the sun itself, you would notice that the light hitting your retina comes from all directions due to scattering by molecules other than pure blackbody radiation effects—meaning our atmosphere scatters blue short-wavelength lengths much more efficiently and thus appears bl
